5. REVERSE ENGINEERING AND CIRCUMVENTION

To the extent permitted by applicable law, the User shall not
reverse engineer, decompile, disassemble, circumvent, or otherwise
attempt to reconstruct proprietary implementation details of the
Platform for the purpose of:

a. bypassing security controls;
b. extracting cryptographic keys, credentials, or protected secrets;
c. defeating repository isolation or access controls;
d. reproducing proprietary components without authorization;
e. creating an unauthorized competing implementation through the
   misuse of protected Confidential Information;
f. compromising the Platform or its Users.

This section does not prohibit acts that applicable law expressly
permits, including legally protected interoperability activities,
lawful security research, or other non-waivable statutory exceptions.

Nothing in this License creates an automatic criminal offense.

Where there is a reasonable basis to suspect unlawful conduct, the
Licensor may preserve relevant evidence and refer the matter to the
competent authorities or courts, as appropriate under applicable law.


6. PROTECTION OF CONFIDENTIAL INFORMATION

Users shall not knowingly obtain, use, disclose, publish, transfer,
or exploit Confidential Information without authorization.

This prohibition includes, without limitation:

a. cryptographic keys and recovery credentials;
b. private repository contents belonging to other Users;
c. non-public server configurations and security mechanisms;
d. internal credentials and authentication material;
e. non-public vulnerability details that expose Users or the Platform
   to unauthorized access or material harm;
f. confidential technical artifacts obtained through unauthorized
   access.

Users must not attempt to infer, extract, or disclose another User's
private data through unauthorized access to the Platform.

Confidentiality obligations do not apply to information that the User
can demonstrate was lawfully known without a confidentiality
obligation, independently developed without misuse of Confidential
Information, or lawfully obtained from an authorized source.

Nothing in this section restricts legally protected disclosures,
mandatory legal reporting, lawful security research, or rights that
cannot be waived under applicable law.


7. PLATFORM AND REPOSITORY INTEGRITY

Changes to official Platform repositories and production
infrastructure may be performed only by authorized maintainers
through approved procedures.

Unauthorized commits, code injections, configuration changes,
repository alterations, or infrastructure modifications are prohibited.

Users retain control over commits and changes within their own
repositories, subject to their permissions and the applicable service
rules.

No User may alter another User's repository without authorization.

No User may introduce malicious code, exploit code, unauthorized
persistence mechanisms, or other material intended to compromise the
Platform or its Users.

External publication, mirroring, redistribution, or commercial
exploitation of proprietary Platform components requires the
applicable authorization, except where such restrictions cannot
lawfully apply.

Nothing in this section prohibits Users from maintaining lawful
independent repositories or making authorized changes to their own
software.


8. PRIVATE REPOSITORY CONFIDENTIALITY

Private Repositories are intended to be accessible only to their
respective owners and explicitly authorized collaborators.

The Platform shall enforce repository access controls according to
its documented security architecture.

Private repository source code shall not be used to train artificial
intelligence models without the explicit authorization of the
relevant repository owner.

Private repository contents shall not be intentionally disclosed to
unauthorized Users.

Encryption, storage isolation, access controls, and key management
shall be implemented according to the Platform's actual technical
design and applicable security commitments.

The existence of a Private Repository does not authorize the
Platform, its maintainers, or other Users to disregard applicable
privacy and confidentiality obligations.

No provision of this License constitutes a guarantee that any system
is immune to all vulnerabilities, compromise, data loss, or attacks.


9. COPILOT AND CENTRAL AI ARCHITECTURE

The Platform distinguishes between account-specific Copilots and
Central AI.

9.1. Account-specific Copilot

Each User's Copilot is intended to analyze code and technical
information within the scope authorized for that User.

The Copilot may identify potential problems and report them to the
User.

The Copilot may transmit relevant technical artifacts to Central AI
for further analysis, in accordance with the Platform's documented
architecture and privacy commitments.

9.2. Central AI

Central AI assists with internal Platform processes and may analyze
technical artifacts, diagnostic information, structural representations,
and other authorized inputs to assess errors and formulate proposed
solutions.

Central AI is not intended to directly read the source code of
Private Repositories.

Central AI may use structural analysis tools, including AST, CST,
and other appropriate analysis mechanisms, to assess technical
problems and prepare candidate solutions.

The actual information available to Central AI shall be limited by
the Platform's implemented access controls and data-flow restrictions.

9.3. User authorization

Central AI and the Copilot do not possess autonomous authority to
modify a User's repository.

Proposed patches or other repository modifications must be presented
to the User for explicit authorization before being applied.

No AI-generated proposal constitutes permission to modify a
repository, change its contents, or perform an otherwise restricted
operation.

This requirement does not prevent previously authorized, user-configured
operations that are explicitly covered by the applicable service rules.

9.4. Neural-network interconnection

The Platform may interconnect or share specified neural-network
computational capabilities across account-specific Copilots while
maintaining account-specific vocabularies and designated data boundaries.

Such interconnection does not, by itself, grant a Copilot or Central
AI permission to access another User's repository.

Shared computational components must operate within the Platform's
documented data-flow, authorization, and confidentiality constraints.

Vocabulary separation and tensor-level computational separation
shall not be represented as absolute information-isolation guarantees
unless supported by the implemented architecture and appropriate
verification.

9.5. Model training

Private source code shall not be used to train AI models without
explicit authorization from the relevant repository owner.

Feedback may be used to improve Copilot functionality in accordance
with the applicable privacy policy and the User's choices.

Feedback collection and model improvement must not be used as a
means to circumvent the prohibition on unauthorized training with
private source code.


10. SECURITY VULNERABILITY DISCLOSURE

Users who discover a security vulnerability affecting the Platform
are requested to report it through the designated confidential
security reporting channel.

Reports should provide sufficient technical information for
reproduction, assessment, and remediation without unnecessarily
exposing private data or compromising other systems.

The maintainers will assess reported vulnerabilities internally and
determine appropriate remediation and disclosure measures.

Users must not exploit a vulnerability to access unauthorized data,
compromise another account, disrupt services, or obtain Confidential
Information.

Unauthorized disclosure of credentials, private data, exploitable
secrets, or vulnerability details that materially endanger the
Platform or its Users may result in enforcement action where legally
justified.

The project may pursue available civil or criminal remedies where
the facts and applicable law support such action.

Nothing in this section prohibits lawful security research, legally
protected disclosures, reporting to competent authorities, or
disclosures required by law.

The Platform's internal vulnerability-handling policy does not
eliminate any reporting obligations imposed by applicable law.


11. USER RESPONSIBILITIES

Users are responsible for:

a. maintaining the confidentiality of their credentials;
b. securing their recovery mechanisms;
c. using the Platform only within their authorized permissions;
d. obtaining authorization before accessing another person's data;
e. ensuring that uploaded materials may lawfully be stored and used;
f. reviewing proposed AI-generated changes before authorizing them;
g. complying with applicable law and the service terms.

Users must not upload or distribute malicious material intended to
compromise the Platform or other Users.

Users must not misrepresent their authorization to access repositories,
accounts, infrastructure, or confidential materials.


12. TRANSPARENCY AND PUBLICATION

The project aims to provide transparency regarding publicly released
source code, documented security practices, and material changes to
the Platform.

A component explicitly published under an open-source license is
governed by that license.

A component published without an explicit open-source license remains
subject to its applicable proprietary rights and restrictions.

Public visibility does not, by itself, grant permission to redistribute,
modify, sublicense, or commercially exploit a proprietary component.

Security-sensitive information may be withheld where publication
would expose credentials, private data, exploitable secrets, or
materially increase risks to Users or infrastructure.

The project shall not describe a component as open source unless the
applicable licensing terms satisfy the relevant definition of
open-source software.


